Behaviour steering in refurbishment design refers to the intentional use of structural, informational, and contextual elements that guide users toward sustainable choices without restricting freedom. Unlike mandates or prohibitions, steering approaches make preferred actions easier, more visible, and more socially valued while maintaining user agency. In refurbishment contexts, this might involve designing friction into disposal pathways while streamlining repair and resale options, providing transparent information about product longevity and refurbishment quality, or establishing social norms around product care and reuse.

Effective behavior steering requires understanding user motivations and decision-making contexts. Price signals, default options, convenience of access, and social proof all play roles in shaping whether consumers engage with refurbished products or repair services. Digital platforms can make refurbishment options prominent and convenient, while design language can communicate reliability and quality. This approach to circular business models recognizes that sustainable systems are built through many small design decisions that collectively reshape consumption patterns rather than relying on individual environmental consciousness alone.